  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe aforementioned weak mid-level low over west Texas will become\na little better defined by Saturday morning and will help \ncontinue to draw in low to mid-level moisture into OK and western \nAR. Isolated to scattered light to moderate rainfall may be \nongoing across portions of southeast OK and into northwest AR at \nthe start of the long-term period. CAMs show very light and \nspotty, off/on rain showers lingering south of I-40 through the \ndaytime Saturday. Most locations should remain dry. Cloud cover is\nexpected to stay somewhat broken to overcast on Saturday, keeping\ntemperatures unseasonably cool; high temperatures will reach the \nmid to upper 70s. \n\nThe weak mid-level low will drift south and east across north TX\nSaturday into Sunday, with the trough axis sliding east of the\narea around midday Sunday. Dry and mostly pleasant weather will\nprevail through the upcoming workweek next week. \n\nTemperatures this weekend will feel every bit of autumn,\nespecially on Saturday with high temperatures generally reaching \nthe mid to upper 70s everywhere. Global models indicate a weak and\nsubtle cold front will work its way across the area Sunday into \nMonday. The absence of any significant moisture will keep things \nhigh and dry as it moves through. The front may drop daytime \ntemperatures a degree or two on Monday, compared to Sunday. The \neffects of the front may be felt more during the evenings and\nearly mornings when low temperatures dip into the upper 40s and \n50s both Monday morning and Tuesday morning. Despite the weak \nfront on Sunday, high temperatures will slowly and gradually \nmoderate back above average and into the 80s by Tuesday, once \nhigh pressure shifts east and southerly surface winds make a \nreturn. Guidance even brings back widespread mid to upper 80s and \nperhaps even lower 90s south of I-40 by mid to late week. \n\nMejia",
